---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
import { DOWNLOADS, DOWNLOADS_ENABLED } from '../../site';

const win = DOWNLOADS.find((d) => d.id === 'windows')!;
const mac = DOWNLOADS.find((d) => d.id === 'macos')!;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="install"
  description="Install Krova on Windows or macOS: the SmartScreen and Gatekeeper warnings, macOS permissions, finding the tray icon, and how to uninstall and remove all of Krova's data."
  lead="Install Krova on Windows or macOS, find its icon, and remove it completely if you change your mind. Both builds are unsigned, so read the warnings first."
>
  {
    !DOWNLOADS_ENABLED && (
      <div class="note note--warn">
        <p>
          <strong>The first release is not published yet.</strong> These steps describe what the installers do. They
          were written from the installer&rsquo;s build script and the app&rsquo;s code, not from watching other
          people install it.
        </p>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="windows">Windows 10 and 11</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Get <code>{win.file}</code> from the <a href={url('/download/')}>Download page</a>. It is a 64-bit installer.
      You can <a href={url('/download/#verify')}>check its hash</a> first.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run it. Windows shows <strong>Windows protected your PC</strong>, because the installer is not signed. Select
      <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Follow the installer. It installs for your user only and does not ask for administrator rights. If Microsoft&rsquo;s
      WebView2 runtime is missing, it downloads it, which needs an internet connection once.
    </li>
    <li>
      On the last page, leave <strong>Run Krova</strong> ticked, and tick <strong>Create desktop shortcut</strong> if you
      want one. Otherwise start Krova from the Start menu.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="tray">Find Krova</h2>
  <p>
    Krova starts hidden: no window opens, and it has no taskbar button. Look for its icon in the notification area at the
    right end of the taskbar. Windows often puts new icons behind the <strong>^</strong> arrow (&ldquo;Show hidden
    icons&rdquo;); drag the Krova icon out of there to keep it visible.
  </p>
  <p>Right-click the icon (on a Mac, click it) to open the menu:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Enabled</strong>: tick to turn Krova on. When off, dragging windows never shows zones.</li>
    <li><strong>Edit layouts&hellip;</strong>: opens the layout editor.</li>
    <li><strong>Settings&hellip;</strong>: opens the settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Launch at login</strong>: start Krova when you sign in.</li>
    <li><strong>Quit Krova</strong>: stops it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Closing Krova&rsquo;s window only hides it; the app keeps running in the tray. Starting Krova a second time does not
    open a second copy: it brings the first one&rsquo;s window forward.
  </p>

  <h2 id="macos">macOS 13 and later</h2>
  <div class="note note--warn">
    <p><strong>Never tested on a real Mac.</strong> The macOS build is unsigned, not notarised, and has not been run on a Mac. Expect problems, and treat what follows as the intended behaviour.</p>
  </div>
  <ol>
    <li>Get <code>{mac.file}</code> from the <a href={url('/download/')}>Download page</a>. It runs on Apple silicon and Intel.</li>
    <li>Open the disk image and drag <strong>Krova</strong> to <strong>Applications</strong>.</li>
    <li>
      The first launch is blocked by Gatekeeper. In Applications, right-click (or Control-click) <strong>Krova</strong>,
      choose <strong>Open</strong>, then <strong>Open</strong> again. If the dialog has no Open button, go to System
      Settings, Privacy &amp; Security, scroll to Security, and choose <strong>Open Anyway</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Grant <strong>Accessibility</strong>: System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security, Accessibility, and turn on
      <strong>Krova</strong>. If Krova is already listed, switch it off and on again. Krova shows a banner in its window
      until access is granted. If snapping still does nothing, quit Krova and start it again.
    </li>
    <li>
      macOS may also ask for <strong>Input Monitoring</strong>, so that Krova can notice a window being dragged. Krova
      only watches the left mouse button and whether your activation key and Escape are held; it does not read what you type.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Krova lives in the menu bar and has no Dock icon. The menu is the same as on Windows.</p>

  <h2 id="uninstall">Uninstall and remove all your data</h2>
  <h3>Windows</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose <strong>Quit Krova</strong> from the tray menu.</li>
    <li>Open Settings, Apps, Installed apps, find <strong>Krova</strong> and choose Uninstall.</li>
    <li>
      On the first page of the uninstaller, tick <strong>Delete the application data</strong>, then continue. That
      removes both data folders below. If you leave it unticked, they stay.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>This is everything Krova can leave on a Windows PC:</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Where Krova keeps data on Windows">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">What</th><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Removed by the uninstaller</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Settings and layouts (<code>config.json</code>, and <code>config.json.bak</code> if it was ever recovered)</td>
          <td><code>%APPDATA%\dev.krova.desktop</code></td>
          <td>Only with &ldquo;Delete the application data&rdquo;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The web view&rsquo;s cache and browser profile (WebView2)</td>
          <td><code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\dev.krova.desktop</code></td>
          <td>Only with &ldquo;Delete the application data&rdquo;</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The start-up entry, if Launch at login was on</td>
          <td>Value <code>Krova</code> under <code>HKEY_CURRENT_USER\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code></td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The program, Start menu and desktop shortcuts</td>
          <td><code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Krova</code></td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>To remove the data by hand, quit Krova first, then run this in PowerShell:</p>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:APPDATA\dev.krova.desktop" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\dev.krova.desktop" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-ItemProperty -Path "HKCU:\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run" -Name "Krova" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue</code></pre>

  <h3>macOS</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Turn off <strong>Launch at login</strong> in Krova, then choose <strong>Quit Krova</strong>.</li>
    <li>Drag <strong>Krova</strong> from Applications to the Bin.</li>
    <li>Delete <code>~/Library/Application Support/dev.krova.desktop</code> (your settings and layouts).</li>
    <li>
      If a start-up entry is left, remove it in System Settings, General, Login Items, or delete
      <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.krova.desktop.plist</code> if it exists.
    </li>
    <li>Remove Krova from the Accessibility list (and Input Monitoring, if it is there) in Privacy &amp; Security.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="muted">
    The list of what Krova stores, and why, is on the <a href={url('/privacy/')}>Privacy page</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
